From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 02:38:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media: apple: isp: recover from a timed-out P010 command

A P010 command or buffer batch that timed out set ipc_failed. From then
on every command failed with -EIO, and stopping the stream halted the
coprocessor. T6040 firmware is resident and cannot be started again, so
one timeout left the camera unusable until the next system boot.

The latch guards against a real race. A timed-out request still owns
its ring slot, and every request on the IO and BUF_H2T rings carries the
same arg0, the command area address. A late answer would be taken for
the answer to the next request written to that slot, and the firmware
would then expect that request in the following slot. The next request
would also overwrite the command area while the firmware may still read
the old one.

Keep the timed-out channel in ipc_stale instead. Before anything else
writes the command area or a ring, isp_ipc_resync() rings the doorbell
once more and waits up to one second for the late answer. If it comes,
it is consumed, the cursor moves on and the ring is in step again, so
the next command and the next stream work. If it does not, ipc_failed
is set as before and the stream stop halts the firmware before its
buffer leases are released. Both outcomes are logged once.

Stopping the camera resolves a pending timeout the same way before it
frees anything the firmware may still use. A buffer batch that timed
out in buf_queue now errors the queue and keeps its leases until the
stream stops, instead of halting the firmware straight away. An
answered but malformed reply leaves the ring in step, so it fails only
its own command. A fresh firmware boot clears both flags.

Add KUnit cases for a late answer, for no answer at all and for a late
ACK that must not answer the next request.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
(cherry picked from commit 504cb5d4f20cdef8e550a3c12dd12635ca248f8d)
